# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 001
ps("V02-L-001", "Arts and literature",
   "Keeping translators off book covers conceals the person who shaped the prose, at a cost to readers and translators.",
   "Medium",
   "Publishers often keep the translator's name off the cover of a translated novel, on the theory that readers want to feel they are meeting the author directly and that a second name reminds them of an intermediary. The reasoning is understandable, but it rests on a fiction. Every sentence the reader of a translation encounters was chosen by the translator: the rhythm, the register, the jokes that land or fall flat. To hide that person is not to remove the intermediary but to conceal one who is doing a great deal of the work, and the concealment has costs. Readers who do not know a book is translated cannot judge whether an awkward phrase belongs to the author or to the rendering, and translators whose names are buried find it harder to build the reputations that would let them command better fees and better projects. Naming the translator does not spoil the illusion of direct contact so much as make the reader's trust an informed one.",
   "Omitting the translator from the cover conceals, rather than removes, the person who chose every sentence, which hampers readers' judgement and translators' careers; naming them makes trust informed.",
   [("Because their names are hidden on covers, translators struggle to build reputations and so cannot command the fees and the projects that their considerable work on each novel deserves.",
     "This is one of the two costs listed near the end; it leaves out the central claim that hiding the translator conceals rather than removes an intermediary, and the cost to readers."),
    ("A translated novel is really the translator's book rather than the author's, so the translator's name ought to replace, or at least precede, the author's name on the cover.",
     "The paragraph says the translator does 'a great deal of the work', not that the book belongs to the translator; it asks only that the translator be named, not ranked above the author."),
    ("Publishers hide translators' names because readers dislike translated books, and this preference, though understandable, steadily lowers the quality of the translations that publishers commission.",
     "The publishers' theory is that readers want to feel they meet the author directly, not that they dislike translations, and nothing is said about falling translation quality.")],
   "The paragraph rejects the publishers' reasoning as resting 'on a fiction': the translator chose every sentence, so hiding the name 'is not to remove the intermediary but to conceal one'. It names two costs (readers cannot tell author from rendering; translators cannot build reputations) and ends by saying naming makes trust 'an informed one'. The correct answer holds the central claim, both costs and the conclusion.",
   "Choosing the option about translators' careers because it is concrete and true to the paragraph, without noticing that it is only one of two supporting costs.",
   "V02L-translator-credit-covers",
   "The narrow distractor is accurate and specific, so the correct answer must be preferred on scope rather than truth.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 002
ps("V02-L-002", "Arts and literature",
   "Abridged classics remove what makes a book distinctive and may deter young readers from the original.",
   "Easy",
   "Abridged editions of classic novels are often defended as a gentle doorway: a twelve-year-old who would abandon an eight-hundred-page original might finish a hundred-page version and come back for the real thing later. The trouble is that what abridgement removes is rarely random. Editors cut digressions, descriptions and the slow passages in which characters think, because these are what make a book long, but they are also what make it the particular book it is. What survives is the plot, and plot is the part of a classic that a film or a summary could deliver just as well. A young reader who finishes such a version may therefore conclude that they have read the book, and that it was merely a story, and see no reason to return. The doorway can easily become a wall.",
   "Defended as a route to the originals, abridged classics tend to keep only plot and cut what makes each book distinctive, so young readers may see no reason to return.",
   [("Children should not be given classic novels until they are old enough to read the complete originals, since the shortened editions are written by editors rather than by the authors themselves.",
     "The paragraph makes no recommendation about when children should read classics, and its objection is to what abridgement removes, not to who wrote the shortened text."),
    ("Abridged editions are valuable because they let young readers finish classic stories they would otherwise abandon, and many of these readers later go on to read the full versions.",
     "This is the defence the paragraph reports in its first sentence and then rejects; the author argues that the doorway 'can easily become a wall'."),
    ("Editors of abridged classics choose their cuts carelessly and at random, which is why the shortened versions of great novels so often fail to hold the interest of young readers.",
     "The paragraph says what abridgement removes 'is rarely random': editors systematically cut digressions and description. It does not claim the versions fail to hold interest.")],
   "The paragraph states the defence (abridgements as a 'doorway'), then argues that the cuts remove exactly what makes a book 'the particular book it is', leaving plot, so a reader may think they have read the book and 'see no reason to return'. The correct answer keeps the defence, the mechanism and the consequence.",
   "Picking the option that restates the opening defence, which the paragraph sets up only to answer.",
   "V02L-abridged-classics",
   "The turn ('The trouble is') is explicit and each distractor is clearly off once it is read.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 003
ps("V02-L-003", "Arts and literature",
   "Laugh tracks persist because laughter is social and they supply company to a viewer watching alone.",
   "Medium",
   "Recorded laughter on television comedies is often mocked as a crude instruction to the audience, a machine telling viewers when something is funny. Yet its persistence for decades suggests it answers a real need. Laughter is overwhelmingly a social behaviour: people laugh far more readily in company than alone, and they often laugh less at a joke than at the shared recognition that a joke has been made. A viewer watching a comedy alone at home is deprived of that company, and the track supplies a substitute, a sense of sitting among others who are enjoying the same thing. This explains why the track feels most intrusive to viewers who are themselves watching in a group, since they already have the company it imitates.",
   "Recorded laughter lasts because laughing is largely social: it gives a solitary viewer a stand-in for an audience, which is also why it irritates people already watching together.",
   [("Recorded laughter is a crude device that tells audiences when to laugh, and viewers have come to resent being instructed in this way by the people who make television comedies.",
     "This is the mockery the paragraph reports and then answers with 'Yet'; the author explains the device as meeting a real need, not as an instruction viewers resent."),
    ("People laugh far more readily in company than when alone, and they often laugh more at the shared recognition that a joke has been made than at the joke itself.",
     "This is the premise about laughter, not the point; it says nothing about laugh tracks, the solitary viewer or the group viewer."),
    ("Recorded laughter suits viewers who watch comedies in groups, since it adds to the shared enjoyment they already feel, while solitary viewers find the device intrusive and false.",
     "This reverses the paragraph, which says the track supplies company to the viewer alone and feels 'most intrusive' to those watching in a group.")],
   "After reporting the common mockery, the paragraph argues that laugh tracks persist because laughter is social; the track gives a viewer alone 'a sense of sitting among others', and this also explains why groups find it intrusive. The correct answer states the explanation and its corollary.",
   "Choosing the reversal because both groups are mentioned, without tracking which group the track is said to help.",
   "V02L-sitcom-laugh-tracks",
   "One distractor reverses the two groups and another states only the premise, so the reader must track the direction of the explanation.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 004
ps("V02-L-004", "Arts and literature",
   "Sampling cannot be called uncreative merely because its materials pre-exist, though quality and payment remain open.",
   "Difficult",
   "Critics of sampling in popular music often argue that a producer who builds a track from fragments of other people's recordings is not composing at all but merely assembling, and that the result borrows its value from the originals. The argument assumes that originality lies in producing sounds no one has produced before. On that test, however, very little music of any period would pass: composers have always worked with inherited scales, forms, rhythms and even melodies, and much admired music is openly built on borrowed tunes. What distinguishes a composer is not the novelty of the materials but what is done with them, the choices of selection, placement and transformation that make a familiar element mean something new. This does not settle whether any particular sampled track is good, or whether the original performers deserve payment; those are separate questions. It shows only that sampling cannot be dismissed as uncreative merely because its raw materials already exist.",
   "The charge that sampling is uncreative locates originality in new materials, a test most music would fail; creativity lies in transforming materials, though this leaves a track's quality and payment unsettled.",
   [("Sampling is as creative as any other way of composing, so sampled tracks deserve the same respect as wholly original works and their producers owe nothing to the performers they borrow from.",
     "The paragraph says explicitly that whether performers 'deserve payment' is a separate question it does not settle, and it does not claim every sampled track deserves respect."),
    ("Because composers in every period have borrowed scales, forms and melodies, no music has ever been truly original, and critics should abandon the idea of originality altogether.",
     "The paragraph relocates originality in 'what is done with' materials rather than discarding it; it never says no music is original."),
    ("Whether a sampled track is creative depends on how good it is and whether the original performers were paid for their work, not on how new its raw materials happen to be.",
     "The paragraph calls quality and payment 'separate questions' from creativity; this option turns them into the test of creativity, which distorts the argument.")],
   "The paragraph identifies the critics' assumption (originality means new sounds), shows that almost no music meets it, relocates originality in 'selection, placement and transformation', and then limits its own conclusion: quality and payment are 'separate questions'. The correct answer keeps the refutation, the positive account and the limit.",
   "Choosing the option that mentions quality and payment, because those words appear in the paragraph, without noticing that it makes them criteria of creativity rather than separate questions.",
   "V02L-sampling-music-originality",
   "Two distractors reuse the paragraph's qualification and its examples while distorting their role; the correct answer depends on reading the final two sentences as a limit on the claim.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 005
ps("V02-L-005", "Language and media",
   "The blanket rule against the passive overstates a narrow objection; the passive has real uses.",
   "Medium",
   "Style manuals routinely tell writers to avoid the passive voice, and the advice has hardened into a rule that many writers apply without thinking. The case against the passive is real but narrow. A passive sentence can hide the agent ('mistakes were made'), and a long run of passives can make prose feel inert. But the passive also does work that nothing else does as neatly. It lets a writer keep the topic of a paragraph in the subject position, so that a passage about a disease can go on talking about the disease ('it is spread by mosquitoes') rather than lurching to whatever happens to cause it. It lets scientists foreground a procedure rather than the person who carried it out, when the person is irrelevant. The useful question is never whether a sentence is passive but whether the reader needs to know who acted, and what the sentence should be about.",
   "The rule against the passive inflates a narrow objection: passives can hide agents or deaden prose but also keep topics in focus, so the reader's needs should decide.",
   [("Writers should generally prefer the passive voice to the active, because it keeps the topic of a passage in subject position and directs attention to procedures rather than people.",
     "The paragraph defends the passive for particular purposes and accepts the case against it as 'real'; it never says the passive should generally be preferred."),
    ("The passive voice serves mainly to conceal responsibility, as in 'mistakes were made', and style manuals are right to warn writers against using it in serious prose.",
     "Concealing the agent is the 'real but narrow' case against the passive; the paragraph goes on to describe work the passive does well, so endorsing the rule reverses it."),
    ("Scientific writing differs from other prose because it describes procedures, so the passive voice, though unsuitable in most writing, is perfectly acceptable in laboratory reports.",
     "Science is one example of the passive's uses; the paragraph also gives the topic-keeping use for any passage and does not call the passive unsuitable elsewhere.")],
   "The paragraph grants the objection ('real but narrow'), lists the passive's uses (keeping the topic in subject position, foregrounding procedure) and ends by replacing the rule with a question about what the reader needs. The correct answer captures concession, counter-case and the replacement test.",
   "Choosing the option about science, which is a vivid example, and missing that it narrows a general point.",
   "V02L-passive-voice-advice",
   "The correct answer must balance the concession against the defence; two distractors each keep only one side.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 006
ps("V02-L-006", "Language and media",
   "An unaltered news photograph is still shaped by its cropping, so readers should ask what the frame excludes.",
   "Easy",
   "A news photograph is often treated as the most neutral element of a report: the words may spin, but the camera simply records. Yet every published photograph has been cropped, and cropping is a choice about what the event was. A picture of a single protester shouting at a line of police tells one story; the same frame widened to show a quiet crowd of hundreds behind him tells another, and the wider frame may show that the shouting man was alone. Nothing in either version is false, and no pixel has been altered. The distortion, if there is one, lies entirely in the boundary. That is why a reader who wants to judge a photograph should ask not only what it shows but what its edges leave out.",
   "An unaltered news photograph is still shaped by cropping, since the frame's edges decide what the event seems to be, so readers should ask what lies beyond them.",
   [("News photographs are less trustworthy than written reports, because photographers routinely alter their images to make protests look more dramatic and violent than the events really were.",
     "The paragraph stresses that 'no pixel has been altered' and does not rank photographs below written reports; the issue is the frame, not manipulation."),
    ("A photograph of one protester shouting at police can make a peaceful demonstration of hundreds of people look violent and disorderly to those who see it in a newspaper.",
     "This is the illustration only; it omits the general point that cropping, not falsification, shapes what any news photograph seems to show."),
    ("Newspapers ought to publish every photograph uncropped, so that readers can see the whole scene for themselves and decide on their own what really happened at the event.",
     "The paragraph prescribes nothing for newspapers; its advice is to readers, to ask what the edges leave out.")],
   "The paragraph challenges the view that photographs are neutral: cropping 'is a choice about what the event was', the distortion 'lies entirely in the boundary', and readers should ask what the edges leave out. The correct answer states the point, the reason and the advice.",
   "Picking the protester example because it is the most memorable sentence.",
   "V02L-cropping-news-photographs",
   "The thesis and the advice are stated outright; distractors are an example, an extreme claim and an invented prescription.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 007
ps("V02-L-007", "Language and media",
   "The audiobook debate should turn on control of pace and rereading, which matters by kind of text.",
   "Difficult",
   "Whether listening to an audiobook counts as reading is usually argued as a question of status, with defenders insisting that the story reaches the mind either way and sceptics treating listening as a lesser substitute. Both sides tend to miss what actually differs. For narrative of moderate difficulty, a competent listener appears to follow and remember the story about as well as a reader does, so the sceptic's claim that listening is shallower is hard to sustain as a general rule. The difference lies in control. A reader sets the pace, slows down at a difficult sentence, glances back to check who said what, and lets the eye run ahead; a listener receives the text at the narrator's speed and must make an effort to go back. For a thriller this hardly matters. For a dense argument, where comprehension depends on rereading, it may matter a great deal. The useful question is therefore not whether listening is reading, but what kind of text is being taken in.",
   "Listening is not generally shallower than reading, but it gives less control over pace and going back, a difference that matters little for stories and possibly much for dense argument.",
   [("Audiobooks are an inferior substitute for printed books, since listeners cannot slow down or glance back, and so they understand and remember less of whatever they hear.",
     "The paragraph says the claim that listening is shallower 'is hard to sustain as a general rule'; loss of control matters mainly for dense argument, not for whatever is heard."),
    ("Listening to an audiobook is a form of reading, since the story reaches a listener's mind just as it reaches a reader's, so the debate about its status is pointless.",
     "This is the defenders' position, which the paragraph says misses 'what actually differs'; the author insists on a real difference in control."),
    ("Readers and listeners differ mainly in speed, since narrators read faster than most people, so audiobooks suit thrillers better than slow and descriptive literary fiction.",
     "The difference named is control over pace and rereading, not that narrators are faster, and the contrast drawn is between stories and dense argument, not between kinds of fiction.")],
   "The paragraph rejects both sides of the status debate: listening is not generally shallower (against the sceptics), but there is a real difference, 'control' (against the defenders), which 'hardly matters' for a thriller and 'may matter a great deal' for dense argument. The correct answer keeps both corrections and the dependence on the kind of text.",
   "Choosing the defenders' option because the paragraph agrees that listeners follow stories well, and missing that it also insists on a real difference.",
   "V02L-audiobooks-as-reading",
   "The paragraph corrects both camps; each of two distractors is one camp's view and partly matches the text.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

