ps("V02-L-008", "Language and media",
   "A correlation between texting and good spelling cannot show benefit but does count against the feared harm.",
   "Medium",
   "Teachers and parents have long worried that the abbreviations of text messaging, the dropped vowels and phonetic shortcuts, would corrode children's spelling. Suppose a school compares its pupils and finds that the heaviest users of such abbreviations are, if anything, slightly better spellers than the rest. It would be tempting to conclude that texting improves spelling, but the finding cannot carry that weight. To abbreviate a word playfully a child must usually know how it is spelled in full, so skill with abbreviations may simply be a sign of a comfort with language that the better spellers already had. What the finding does undermine is the fear itself: if abbreviating were steadily eroding spelling, the heaviest abbreviators should be doing worse, not better. A correlation that cannot prove benefit can still be evidence against harm.",
   "That heavy abbreviators spell slightly better cannot show texting helps spelling, since good spellers may simply abbreviate more, but it does tell against the fear that texting harms it.",
   [("Text messaging improves children's spelling, because abbreviating words playfully requires them to know the full spellings, and the habit keeps reinforcing that knowledge every day.",
     "The paragraph calls this conclusion 'tempting' and says the finding 'cannot carry that weight'; knowing full spellings is offered as a reason good spellers abbreviate, not as a benefit of texting."),
    ("The worry about texting and spelling has now been disproved, so schools ought to stop discouraging pupils from using abbreviations in their messages and other informal writing.",
     "The paragraph says the finding does 'undermine' the fear and counts as 'evidence against harm', which is weaker than disproof, and it recommends nothing to schools."),
    ("Correlations between children's habits and their abilities can never tell us anything at all about causes, so a school's comparison of its own pupils is of no value in this debate.",
     "The paragraph's last sentence says such a correlation 'can still be evidence against harm', which contradicts the claim that it is of no value.")],
   "The paragraph uses a hypothetical finding to make a point about evidence: it cannot show texting helps (better spellers may abbreviate more), but it counts against the feared harm, since harm would predict the opposite pattern. The correct answer keeps both halves of this asymmetry.",
   "Choosing the 'disproved' option because the paragraph does undermine the fear, and missing the gap between evidence against a claim and disproof of it.",
   "V02L-text-abbreviations-literacy",
   "The point is an asymmetry about evidence; distractors take one half too far.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 009
ps("V02-L-009", "Education",
   "Interesting but irrelevant textbook asides can impair learning, unlike vivid material that carries the idea.",
   "Medium",
   "Textbook writers often add vivid asides to dry material: an anecdote about a scientist's eccentricities in a chapter on electricity, a dramatic photograph of lightning beside a diagram of charge. The intention is to hold interest, and readers do report finding such pages more enjoyable. Yet experiments on learning from text have repeatedly suggested that interesting but irrelevant details can reduce how much of the central explanation students remember and can apply, apparently by drawing attention and memory towards themselves. This does not mean that interest is the enemy of learning. Details that are vivid and also carry the core idea, such as an example that shows the principle at work, do not seem to carry the same cost. The lesson is that engagement should be built from the material that is to be learned, not borrowed from material beside it.",
   "Engaging but irrelevant textbook asides can divert attention from the explanation and weaken learning, though vivid material embodying the idea seems not to; interest should come from content.",
   [("Textbooks should be written in plain language without anecdotes, photographs or examples, since anything that makes a page more enjoyable will distract students from what they are meant to learn.",
     "The paragraph says interest is not 'the enemy of learning' and approves vivid examples that show the principle at work, so banning them contradicts it."),
    ("Students enjoy textbook pages with vivid anecdotes and dramatic photographs more than plain pages, and this enjoyment makes them more willing to study the central explanation.",
     "The paragraph grants the enjoyment but says irrelevant details can reduce what students remember of the explanation; the claim of greater willingness to study is not in it."),
    ("Irrelevant details in textbooks harm learning chiefly because they make pages longer, so that students spend less of their limited reading time on the central explanation.",
     "The mechanism suggested is that such details draw 'attention and memory towards themselves', not that they lengthen the page or use up reading time.")],
   "The paragraph reports a finding (irrelevant but interesting details can reduce learning of the main explanation), qualifies it (relevant vivid details do not carry the same cost), and draws the lesson that engagement should come from the material itself. The correct answer holds finding, qualification and lesson.",
   "Choosing the extreme option because the finding is against decorative detail, and ignoring the qualification about vivid examples.",
   "V02L-seductive-details-textbooks",
   "The correct answer depends on keeping the qualification; one distractor is an overstated version of the finding.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 010
ps("V02-L-010", "Education",
   "Open-book examinations shift the demand from recall to the use of understanding rather than lowering it.",
   "Easy",
   "Open-book examinations are sometimes dismissed as easy, on the assumption that a student who may consult notes need not have learned anything. Anyone who has sat one knows otherwise. Because the facts are available, the examiner cannot reward their mere recall and must instead ask questions that require using them: applying a principle to an unfamiliar case, weighing two sources, building an argument under time pressure. A student who has not understood the material gains little from the book on the desk, since there is no time to learn a subject in the middle of the paper; such students tend to spend the hour leafing anxiously through pages. Open-book examinations do not lower the bar; they move it from remembering to thinking.",
   "Open-book examinations are not easier but differently demanding: since facts can be looked up, they test the application of understanding, which notes cannot supply in the time available.",
   [("Open-book examinations are easy because students may consult their notes, so they reward the students who take careful notes rather than those who truly understand the subject.",
     "This is the dismissal the paragraph rejects in its second sentence; the author argues that notes help little without understanding."),
    ("Closed-book examinations ought to be abolished, since testing the recall of facts that anyone can look up wastes students' time and teaches them nothing useful.",
     "The paragraph defends open-book examinations but says nothing against closed-book ones and recommends no abolition."),
    ("Students who have not understood a subject spend open-book examinations leafing anxiously through their notes, and so they often fail to finish the paper.",
     "This is one supporting detail, and the claim that such students fail to finish is added; the option misses the main point that the bar moves from remembering to thinking.")],
   "The paragraph rebuts the view that open-book examinations are easy: available facts force questions that require using them, and notes cannot substitute for understanding under time pressure. Its last sentence states the point: the bar moves 'from remembering to thinking'.",
   "Choosing the detail about students leafing through pages because it is concrete.",
   "V02L-open-book-examinations",
   "The conclusion is stated in the last sentence and each distractor is plainly off.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 011
ps("V02-L-011", "Education",
   "The cursive debate confuses the case for handwriting with the weaker case for the joined style.",
   "Difficult",
   "Debates over whether schools should still teach joined-up handwriting are usually framed as a contest between tradition and efficiency: cursive is a heritage skill, keyboards are what adults actually use. The framing obscures the fact that the two sides are often talking about different things. The case for teaching some handwriting is fairly strong, since forming letters by hand appears to help young children learn to recognise them, and much early schoolwork is still done with a pencil. But that case is about handwriting in general, not about the particular joined style, and an argument for one is easily mistaken for an argument for the other. The case for cursive specifically rests on weaker grounds: claims that it is faster, or that it builds discipline, are rarely as well supported as their advocates assume. A school could therefore drop cursive and keep handwriting without any inconsistency.",
   "The cursive debate merges two questions: the fairly strong case for teaching handwriting does not carry over to the joined style, whose particular benefits are less well supported.",
   [("Schools should stop teaching handwriting altogether, because keyboards are what adults actually use and the supposed benefits of cursive are rarely supported by evidence.",
     "The paragraph calls the case for handwriting 'fairly strong' and says a school could keep handwriting while dropping cursive; abandoning handwriting contradicts it."),
    ("Cursive should continue to be taught because forming letters by hand helps young children learn to recognise them, which is a benefit that typing on a keyboard cannot provide.",
     "This makes exactly the error the paragraph identifies: it uses the case for handwriting in general as an argument for the particular joined style."),
    ("The debate over cursive is at bottom a contest between tradition and efficiency, and efficiency should prevail because most adult writing is now done on keyboards.",
     "The paragraph says this framing 'obscures' what is at issue, so presenting the debate in these terms and picking a side misses its point.")],
   "The paragraph's central move is a distinction: the case for handwriting in general is 'fairly strong', but 'an argument for one is easily mistaken for an argument for the other', and the case for cursive specifically 'rests on weaker grounds'. The conclusion follows that a school can drop cursive and keep handwriting. The correct answer states the distinction and the asymmetry of support.",
   "Choosing the pro-cursive option because the letter-recognition point is presented as well supported, and missing that it supports handwriting, not cursive.",
   "V02L-cursive-handwriting-instruction",
   "The tempting distractor uses the paragraph's strongest evidence in exactly the way the paragraph warns against; separating them needs the general versus specific distinction.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 012
ps("V02-L-012", "Education",
   "University league tables imply a single best order by fixing weights that are the compiler's choice.",
   "Medium",
   "University league tables are defended as a service to applicants, who would otherwise have to choose among institutions they know little about. The defence has some force, but it overlooks what a single ranked list does. Each table combines many measures, such as research output, staff numbers per student and graduate salaries, into one score, and the weights given to each are a choice made by the compiler, not a fact about universities. Different weights would produce a different order. An applicant who wants small classes and an applicant who wants a strong research culture are served by different universities, yet both are handed the same list as if it settled which is best. A table that published its components and let readers set the weights would serve applicants better than one that pretends a single verdict exists.",
   "League tables inform applicants, but by merging many measures into one score with weights the compiler chose, they imply one best order where other priorities would rank differently.",
   [("League tables are worthless to applicants, because the measures they combine, such as research output and graduate salaries, have nothing to do with the quality of a university's teaching.",
     "The paragraph grants the defence 'some force' and objects to the single weighted score, not to the measures; it never says they are unrelated to teaching."),
    ("Applicants who want small classes should ignore league tables, since the tables systematically favour universities with strong research over those that give students personal attention.",
     "The paragraph claims no bias toward research; its point is that any fixed weighting serves some applicants' priorities and not others'."),
    ("Compilers of league tables deliberately choose their weights so that famous universities stay at the top, which misleads applicants about where they ought to apply.",
     "The paragraph says the weights are 'a choice made by the compiler', not that they are chosen dishonestly; motives are never discussed.")],
   "The paragraph concedes the tables help applicants, then argues that combining measures with chosen weights produces an order that is not 'a fact about universities', so different applicants are wrongly given one verdict. It proposes publishing components so readers can set weights. The correct answer keeps the concession and the central objection.",
   "Choosing the option about deliberate manipulation, reading 'a choice made by the compiler' as an accusation.",
   "V02L-university-league-tables",
   "Distractors add motives or biases that the paragraph does not assert, and each looks like a natural extension of it.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 013
ps("V02-L-013", "Health and medicine",
   "For ordinary short-term back pain, staying active beats prolonged bed rest, within stated limits.",
   "Easy",
   "For a long time, people who hurt their backs were told to lie down and stay down until the pain passed. Current guidance for ordinary, short-lived low back pain has largely reversed this advice: staying as active as the pain allows tends to bring a quicker recovery than prolonged rest, which can stiffen muscles and make a return to normal life harder. This does not mean that a patient should ignore pain or push through heavy work, and a day or two of reduced activity in the worst phase may be sensible. Nor does the advice apply to back pain caused by serious injury or disease, which needs proper assessment. The change is narrower than a slogan like 'keep moving' suggests, but within its range it is clear.",
   "For ordinary short-term back pain, staying reasonably active usually aids recovery better than long bed rest, though brief rest may help and serious causes need separate assessment.",
   [("People with back pain should keep moving and work through the pain, since rest of any kind stiffens the muscles and delays the patient's return to normal daily activity.",
     "The paragraph says a patient should not 'ignore pain or push through heavy work' and that a day or two of reduced activity may be sensible, so ruling out rest of any kind contradicts it."),
    ("Back pain that is caused by serious injury or disease needs careful assessment by a doctor before a patient decides whether to rest or to stay active.",
     "This is one of the qualifications only; it omits the main point that for ordinary back pain activity helps recovery more than prolonged rest."),
    ("Doctors were wrong for many years to recommend bed rest for back pain, which shows that medical advice about common complaints should not be trusted by patients.",
     "The paragraph reports a change in guidance but draws no conclusion about trusting medical advice; this lesson is outside its scope.")],
   "The paragraph reports a finding (activity aids recovery from ordinary back pain better than prolonged rest) and then limits it twice: brief rest may be sensible, and serious causes are excluded. It closes by saying the change is 'narrower than a slogan' but clear within its range. The correct answer keeps the finding and both limits.",
   "Choosing the 'keep moving' option, which is the slogan the paragraph says overstates the change.",
   "V02L-bed-rest-back-pain",
   "Finding and qualifications are stated plainly; the slogan distractor is explicitly flagged as too broad.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 014
ps("V02-L-014", "Health and medicine",
   "Instant online test results honour patients' claims but can mislead; the issue is whether interpretation comes with them.",
   "Medium",
   "Many health systems now let patients see their test results online as soon as they are ready, often before a doctor has looked at them. Supporters see this as an overdue recognition that the information belongs to the patient; critics point out that a patient may meet an alarming number, or a technical phrase like 'cannot exclude malignancy', alone at midnight with no one to explain it. Both are right about something. Early access removes delays and the paternalism of having a doctor decide what a patient may know, and many patients prefer it even when the news is bad. But a result is not the same as its meaning, and a raw value read without context can frighten patients needlessly or reassure them falsely. The choice is less between access and protection than about whether interpretation arrives with the data or only later.",
   "Immediate online access to test results honours patients' claim to their information, yet unexplained results can mislead or alarm, so the real issue is whether interpretation comes with the data.",
   [("Patients should not see their test results until a doctor has reviewed them, since alarming numbers and technical phrases read alone at night cause needless fear and confusion.",
     "This is the critics' position, which the paragraph says is right only 'about something'; it also credits early access with removing delays and paternalism."),
    ("Most patients prefer to see test results at once, even when the news is bad, so health systems are right to release every result online immediately.",
     "This takes the supporters' side and ignores the paragraph's point that a raw result read without context can frighten or falsely reassure."),
    ("Doctors have been paternalistic about test results because they want to control what patients know, and online access has at last brought this unjustified control to an end.",
     "The paragraph mentions paternalism but attributes no motive to doctors, and it does not treat online access as simply settling the matter.")],
   "The paragraph sets out supporters and critics, says 'Both are right about something', grants the benefits of early access and the risk that 'a result is not the same as its meaning', and reframes the choice: whether interpretation arrives with the data. The correct answer keeps both sides and the reframing.",
   "Choosing the supporters' option because the paragraph lists the benefits of early access first and at length.",
   "V02L-patients-reading-test-results",
   "Two distractors each give one side of a balanced comparison; the correct answer requires the reframing in the last sentence.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 015
ps("V02-L-015", "Health and medicine",
   "Individually cautious monitor alarms can together make wards less safe by teaching staff to discount them.",
   "Medium",
   "Hospital wards are full of monitors that sound an alarm whenever a patient's heart rate, oxygen level or blood pressure crosses a threshold. Each alarm is set cautiously, on the reasonable principle that a missed emergency is worse than a false alert. Yet the sum of these cautious settings can make patients less safe. When most alarms turn out to be harmless, triggered by a patient shifting in bed or a sensor slipping, staff learn, quite rationally, to treat them as background noise, to respond slowly or to silence them. The rare alarm that signals a real crisis then arrives in an environment trained to ignore it. The problem is not careless staff but a system in which each device is designed in isolation, as though it were the only voice in the room.",
   "Cautiously set monitor alarms can together make wards less safe: frequent false alerts teach staff to discount them, a fault of system design, not of careless staff.",
   [("Nurses and doctors on busy wards too often ignore or silence monitor alarms, and they need stricter training so that they respond promptly to every alert they hear.",
     "The paragraph says the problem 'is not careless staff' and calls the staff response rational; it proposes no training."),
    ("Monitor alarms should be set to sound far less often, since in practice a missed emergency does less harm to patients than a steady stream of false alerts.",
     "The paragraph calls it 'reasonable' that a missed emergency is worse than a false alert; it faults the combined effect of the settings, not this principle."),
    ("Most monitor alarms on hospital wards are false, being set off when patients shift in bed or when the sensors attached to their bodies slip out of place.",
     "This is a supporting detail; it leaves out the central point that false alerts train staff to ignore the alarms that matter.")],
   "Each cautious setting is reasonable, but 'the sum' can make patients less safe: harmless alarms teach staff 'quite rationally' to discount them, so real crises are missed. The last sentence places the fault in isolated device design, not staff. The correct answer gives the paradox, the mechanism and where the fault lies.",
   "Blaming the staff, which the paragraph explicitly rules out.",
   "V02L-alarm-fatigue-monitors",
   "The correct answer must capture an aggregate effect of individually sensible choices; one distractor overturns the sensible principle itself.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 016
ps("V02-L-016", "Health and medicine",
   "Rare-disease drug incentives work but, keyed to patient numbers, can be gamed; they need better targeting.",
   "Difficult",
   "Because a rare disease affects few people, a drug that treats it promises a small market, and companies left to market incentives alone have little reason to develop one. Governments in many countries have therefore offered inducements, such as extended periods of exclusive sale, to make such drugs worth developing. The policy has produced treatments for conditions that once had none, and that achievement is real. But an incentive defined by the number of patients can be met in ways its designers did not intend. A common disease can sometimes be divided into narrowly defined subtypes, each rare enough to qualify, and a drug that later proves useful for many conditions may keep the protections it earned for one. The result can be high prices on drugs whose markets are not small at all. The remedy is not to abandon the incentives but to tie them more closely to the problem they were meant to solve.",
   "Rare-disease drug incentives have yielded real treatments, but since they turn on patient numbers they can shield high prices in large markets, so they need closer targeting.",
   [("Incentives for rare-disease drugs mainly let companies charge high prices for medicines with large markets, so they have done more harm than good and ought to be withdrawn.",
     "The paragraph calls the policy's achievement 'real' and says 'The remedy is not to abandon the incentives', which this option directly contradicts."),
    ("Drug companies divide common diseases into rare subtypes to qualify for incentives, which shows that the industry cannot be trusted to develop any medicines responsibly.",
     "The paragraph says a disease 'can sometimes' be divided this way and draws no conclusion about the trustworthiness of the whole industry."),
    ("Without government incentives no company would ever develop a drug for a rare disease, since the small number of patients makes such drugs unprofitable to produce and sell.",
     "This overstates the premise, since the paragraph says only 'little reason', and leaves out the paragraph's main point about how the incentives can be exploited.")],
   "The paragraph explains why incentives exist, grants their achievement, shows how a patient-number test can be met unintentionally (subdivided diseases, drugs later used widely) and concludes that the incentives should be tied more closely to their purpose, not abandoned. The correct answer keeps achievement, flaw and remedy.",
   "Choosing the 'withdraw' option because the paragraph's longest stretch describes abuse, and overlooking the explicit rejection of abandonment.",
   "V02L-orphan-drugs-rare-diseases",
   "The criticism dominates the paragraph, so the tempting distractor follows its weight; only the concession and the final sentence rule it out.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 017
ps("V02-L-017", "Cities and geography",
   "Parking minimums have hidden costs and induce driving, so parking supply should not be fixed by rule.",
   "Medium",
   "Many cities require every new building to provide a minimum number of parking spaces, a rule meant to stop drivers from overwhelming the surrounding streets. The intention is sensible, but the rule carries costs that are easy to overlook because they are hidden in other prices. Parking takes land and money, so the requirement raises the cost of every flat and shop that is built, whether or not its occupants own a car; residents who do not drive in effect subsidise those who do. And by guaranteeing that driving will always be convenient, the rule encourages more of it, partly creating the demand it was meant to accommodate. None of this means a city should have no parking. It means that the amount should be decided by those who build and use it, rather than fixed by a rule that assumes everyone will drive.",
   "Required parking raises building costs for drivers and non-drivers alike and encourages driving, so though some parking is needed, its amount should not be set by rule.",
   [("Cities should ban new parking spaces altogether, because parking uses up land and money and tempts people to drive when they could walk or take public transport instead.",
     "The paragraph says 'None of this means a city should have no parking'; a ban contradicts it, and walking and public transport are never mentioned."),
    ("Residents who do not own cars pay more for their flats, because developers are forced to build parking spaces that only car-owning residents will ever actually use.",
     "This is one of the costs described; it omits the induced driving and the conclusion about who should decide the amount of parking."),
    ("Rules requiring parking in new buildings have failed, because drivers still overwhelm the surrounding streets, which shows that such rules can never achieve their purpose.",
     "The paragraph does not say streets are still overwhelmed; its objection is to hidden costs and induced demand, not to failure at the stated aim.")],
   "The paragraph grants the rule a sensible intention, then names two overlooked effects (higher building costs borne by non-drivers too, and induced driving) and concludes, with a qualification, that parking should be decided by builders and users rather than by a rule. The correct answer holds the two effects, the concession and the conclusion.",
   "Choosing the subsidy option because it is the most striking claim in the paragraph.",
   "V02L-parking-minimum-requirements",
   "The correct answer must include the concession that some parking is needed; the extreme distractor is close in spirit to the critique.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 018
ps("V02-L-018", "Cities and geography",
   "Flagship museums regenerate cities only as part of wider investment; their reputation rests on exceptions.",
   "Difficult",
   "When a declining industrial city commissions a spectacular museum from a famous architect, the hope is that the building will draw visitors, investment and a new image, as a few celebrated examples seem to show. The trouble is that the examples are remembered precisely because they are exceptional. For every city whose flagship became a destination, others built expensive buildings that drew crowds for a season and then struggled to fill their galleries or pay their running costs. The successes, moreover, rarely depended on the building alone: they tended to come where the city was already investing in transport, housing and its existing cultural life, so that the museum crowned a wider change rather than causing it. A spectacular building is not useless. But treated as a shortcut to regeneration rather than as one part of it, it is more likely to become a monument to the hope than an instrument of its fulfilment.",
   "Faith that a showpiece museum will revive a declining city rests on memorable exceptions; successes usually crowned wider investment, so such buildings help as one part of regeneration, not as a shortcut.",
   [("Spectacular museums rarely revive declining cities, since most visitors come only once, so cities should spend on transport and housing instead of on new cultural buildings.",
     "The paragraph says such a building 'is not useless' and objects only to treating it as a shortcut; it does not set museums against such spending, and visitors coming only once is never claimed."),
    ("Cities whose flagship museums succeeded were already investing in transport, housing and culture, which proves that the museums themselves contributed nothing to their revival.",
     "The paragraph says the museum 'crowned a wider change' and 'is not useless'; claiming proof that museums contributed nothing overstates the point about dependence on wider investment."),
    ("Famous architects design museums that cost too much to run, so many cities are left struggling to fill the galleries and to pay for the buildings they commissioned.",
     "The paragraph does not blame architects; the struggling museums are evidence that remembered successes are exceptional, not the main point.")],
   "The paragraph attacks the regeneration hope on two grounds: the famous successes are exceptions (selection), and even they depended on wider investment, the museum crowning rather than causing change. It concedes the building 'is not useless' and concludes against using it as a shortcut. The correct answer keeps both grounds and the concession.",
   "Choosing the 'proves nothing' option because the paragraph says the museum did not cause the change, and missing the gap between 'crowned' and 'contributed nothing'.",
   "V02L-flagship-museum-regeneration",
   "Two distractors are overstatements of claims the paragraph does make; the correct answer turns on its concession and on the difference between crowning and causing.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 019
ps("V02-L-019", "Cities and geography",
   "Freezing an old quarter's buildings can drive out the mixed life that gave it character.",
   "Easy",
   "Protecting an old quarter of a city by freezing its buildings in their present form seems the obvious way to preserve its character. But much of what people value in such places was never a matter of facades alone. It came from the mix of uses, the workshops below flats, the small shops and cheap rooms that let all kinds of people live and work there. Strict rules that forbid any alteration can make buildings expensive to adapt and maintain, so that the craftsmen and small traders move out and the quarter fills with boutique hotels and souvenir shops behind perfectly preserved fronts. Conservation that protects the shell while losing the life it once housed has preserved less than it appears.",
   "Freezing an old quarter's buildings can drive out the mix of people and trades that gave it character, so preserving facades alone may lose what made it valuable.",
   [("Old city quarters should be allowed to change freely and without restriction, since rules against alteration only raise costs and end up serving the owners of boutique hotels and souvenir shops.",
     "The paragraph criticises strict freezing, not all conservation, and it does not recommend letting quarters change freely."),
    ("The character of an old quarter lies in its workshops, small shops and cheap rooms rather than in the appearance of its buildings and streets.",
     "The paragraph says character was 'never a matter of facades alone', not that appearance does not matter; and the option omits the argument about strict rules."),
    ("Boutique hotels and souvenir shops are spoiling the old quarters of many cities, and strict conservation rules are needed to stop such businesses from moving in.",
     "This reverses the paragraph, which says strict rules help bring such businesses in by driving out craftsmen and small traders.")],
   "The paragraph argues that character came from 'the mix of uses', that strict no-alteration rules push out craftsmen and traders, and that conservation which keeps 'the shell' while losing 'the life' preserves less than it seems. The correct answer states this mechanism and conclusion.",
   "Choosing the 'rather than' option, which turns 'not facades alone' into 'not facades at all'.",
   "V02L-heritage-zoning-old-quarters",
   "The argument is short and direct, and the reversal and the prescription are clearly off.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 020
ps("V02-L-020", "Cities and geography",
   "Footbridges go unused because they cost walkers far more effort than crossing at street level.",
   "Easy",
   "Pedestrian footbridges over busy roads are built to keep walkers safe, yet many stand almost unused while people dodge traffic at street level beneath them. This is less a sign of recklessness than of a design that ignores how walking works. A footbridge typically asks a pedestrian to climb a long flight of stairs or a ramp, cross, and descend again, adding minutes and considerable effort to a crossing that would take seconds at street level. For the elderly, people with heavy bags and parents with prams, the climb can be a real obstacle. Pedestrians weigh effort against risk, as drivers do, and when the safe route is much harder than the dangerous one, many will take the risk.",
   "Many footbridges go unused not through recklessness but because they cost far more effort than crossing at street level, and pedestrians weigh effort against risk.",
   [("Pedestrians who cross busy roads beneath footbridges are behaving recklessly and should be fined, since a safe crossing has been built for them at public expense.",
     "The paragraph says the behaviour is 'less a sign of recklessness than of a design that ignores how walking works'; fines are not mentioned."),
    ("Footbridges are particularly hard for elderly people, people carrying heavy bags and parents pushing prams, who find the long stairs and ramps a real obstacle.",
     "This is a supporting detail about some users; it misses the general point about effort against risk for all pedestrians."),
    ("Drivers and pedestrians both weigh effort against risk, which is why accidents are most frequent on the busy roads that footbridges are built to cross.",
     "The paragraph makes no claim about where accidents are most frequent; the comparison with drivers only supports the point about pedestrians.")],
   "The paragraph explains a puzzle (unused footbridges): the cause is design, not recklessness, since the bridge adds much effort to a crossing, and pedestrians weigh effort against risk. The correct answer states the puzzle's explanation.",
   "Choosing the detail about the elderly and parents because it is vivid.",
   "V02L-pedestrian-footbridges",
   "The explanation is signposted in the second sentence; distractors are a reversal, a detail and an unsupported causal claim.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 021
ps("V02-L-021", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Impartialists can justify partiality at the level of practice, at the cost of grounding friendship in the general good.",
   "Difficult",
   "Impartial moral theories hold that everyone's interests count equally, and they seem to imply that I should be no more concerned with my friend's welfare than with a stranger's. Critics reply that a person who really thought this way would be incapable of friendship, since to be someone's friend just is to give their good special weight. The critics are right that friendship involves partiality, but the impartialist need not concede that partiality is therefore unjustified. One can argue impartially that a world in which people form close attachments and favour those they love is better for everyone than a world of detached benefactors, and so endorse partiality at the level of the practice while denying that each act of favouritism must be weighed against strangers' claims. The cost of this reply is that the friend's special claim is defended, in the end, by appeal to the general good, which some will feel misdescribes why we help our friends.",
   "Impartialists can grant that friendship is partial by justifying, on impartial grounds, the practice of favouring loved ones, though grounding a friend's claim in the general good may misdescribe it.",
   [("Because friendship requires giving a friend's good special weight, impartial moral theories cannot be correct, and morality must permit each person to favour those they love.",
     "This is the critics' inference, which the paragraph blocks: it grants that friendship is partial but denies that partiality is 'therefore unjustified' on impartial grounds."),
    ("Impartial theories require people to weigh every act of favouritism toward a friend against the claims of strangers, which makes genuine friendship impossible to sustain.",
     "The impartialist reply described explicitly denies 'that each act of favouritism must be weighed against strangers' claims'."),
    ("A world of close attachments is better for everyone than a world of detached benefactors, so people have no reason at all to be concerned with the welfare of strangers.",
     "The paragraph uses this comparison to justify partiality within an impartial framework, where strangers' interests still count equally; denying any reason to care about strangers reverses that.")],
   "The paragraph reports the impartial view, the critics' objection, and an impartialist reply: justify partiality 'at the level of the practice' by appeal to the general good, without weighing each act. It then names the reply's cost. The correct answer states the reply and its cost.",
   "Choosing the critics' option because the paragraph says 'The critics are right that friendship involves partiality', and missing that it rejects their conclusion.",
   "V02L-partiality-toward-friends",
   "Three positions are in play (theory, critics, reply) and the reply's cost must be kept; distractors each present one stage as the conclusion.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 022
ps("V02-L-022", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Preferring anonymous gifts confuses evidence of motive with the value of a gift.",
   "Medium",
   "It is often said that a gift made anonymously is morally superior to one made publicly, since the anonymous donor cannot be seeking praise and so must be acting from pure generosity. The reasoning confuses evidence with value. Anonymity can be good evidence that a donor is not after recognition, but a public donor is not thereby shown to be after it, and even a donor who enjoys the praise may give for reasons that are mostly good. More importantly, the recipients are fed and housed just the same either way, and a well-publicised gift may persuade others to give, multiplying its effect. There may be cases where anonymity matters, as when publicity would humiliate the person helped. But as a general ranking, the preference for anonymous giving judges a gift by what it reveals about the giver rather than by what it does.",
   "Ranking anonymous gifts higher confuses evidence of motive with a gift's worth; public gifts help recipients equally and may inspire others, though anonymity sometimes protects recipients.",
   [("Public gifts are morally better than anonymous ones, because they encourage other people to give and so multiply the good that each donation does for those in need.",
     "The paragraph rejects the general ranking of anonymous over public giving; it does not set up the reverse ranking, and it allows that anonymity sometimes matters."),
    ("Donors who give anonymously are no more generous than those who give publicly, since everyone who gives to charity enjoys receiving at least some praise for doing so.",
     "The paragraph says anonymity 'can be good evidence' of not seeking recognition, and it never claims every donor enjoys praise."),
    ("Anonymous giving is preferable only when publicity would humiliate the people being helped, which is the one case where the donor's motive really matters morally.",
     "The humiliation case concerns harm to the recipient, not the donor's motive, and the paragraph says 'There may be cases', not that this is the only one.")],
   "The paragraph diagnoses the reasoning ('confuses evidence with value'), argues that recipients benefit equally and publicity may multiply giving, allows exceptions such as humiliation, and concludes that the ranking judges a gift by what it reveals rather than what it does. The correct answer carries the diagnosis, the reasons and the exception.",
   "Choosing the reverse ranking because the paragraph mentions that publicity can multiply giving.",
   "V02L-anonymous-giving-motives",
   "The paragraph rejects a ranking without inverting it; the tempting distractor inverts it.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 023
ps("V02-L-023", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Exceptions show moral rules are not absolute, not that they are dispensable, since presumptions have value.",
   "Difficult",
   "Moral rules such as 'do not lie' or 'keep your promises' are often attacked by pointing to exceptions: surely one may lie to save a life, or break a trivial promise to help someone in danger. If a rule has exceptions, the argument runs, it was never really a rule, and we should simply weigh consequences case by case. But the existence of exceptions shows less than this suggests. A rule can be a strong presumption, one that sets the burden of argument and holds unless something weighty overrides it, and such presumptions are valuable precisely because case-by-case weighing is slow, prone to self-serving bias and impossible for others to rely on. Someone who keeps promises only when calculation favours it is not a person others can plan around. The exceptions show that rules are not absolute; they do not show that rules are dispensable.",
   "Exceptions show only that moral rules are not absolute, not that they are dispensable, since as strong presumptions they guard against slow, self-serving case-by-case judgement and make people reliable.",
   [("Moral rules such as keeping promises must be followed without exception, because a person who breaks one even once to help someone can never again be relied on by others.",
     "The paragraph grants that rules 'are not absolute' and accepts the exceptions; it says only that someone who keeps promises only when calculation favours it is unreliable."),
    ("Since one may lie to save a life, moral rules are really summaries of past calculations, and in each new case we should weigh the consequences afresh.",
     "This is the attack the paragraph reports and rejects; it argues that case-by-case weighing is slow, biased and impossible to rely on."),
    ("Case-by-case moral judgement is always self-serving, so people should keep to moral rules even in emergencies where breaking a rule would save a life.",
     "The paragraph says such judgement is 'prone to' bias, not always biased, and it accepts lying to save a life as a legitimate exception.")],
   "The paragraph reports an argument from exceptions to case-by-case weighing, then replies that rules can be strong presumptions whose value lies in avoiding slow, biased and unpredictable judgement. Its last sentence distinguishes 'not absolute' from 'dispensable'. The correct answer keeps the distinction and the reason.",
   "Choosing the absolutist option because the paragraph defends rules, and missing that it concedes they have exceptions.",
   "V02L-moral-rules-exceptions",
   "The answer turns on the distinction between 'not absolute' and 'dispensable'; one distractor collapses it in each direction.",
   cognitive="Analysis", exams=HARD)

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 024
ps("V02-L-024", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Consent given under desperate conditions counts, but cannot by itself justify the terms.",
   "Medium",
   "Consent is usually treated as transforming what would otherwise be wrong into something permissible: the surgeon's knife, the borrowed car, the signed contract. But the transforming power of consent depends on the conditions under which it is given. A worker who accepts dangerous conditions because the alternative is destitution has agreed, in a sense, and it would be odd to say his signature means nothing. Yet when one party can set the terms and the other can only take them or starve, the agreement shows mainly that the worker preferred this bargain to disaster, not that the terms were fair. To treat such consent as settling the matter is to let the stronger party borrow the moral authority of a free choice that was barely available. Consent remains important; it simply cannot do all the justifying work on its own.",
   "Consent's power to permit depends on its conditions: agreement forced by desperation shows a preference over disaster, not fairness, so it counts but cannot justify the terms alone.",
   [("Agreements made by workers facing destitution are not real consent at all, and contracts signed under such pressure ought to be treated by the courts as void and unenforceable.",
     "The paragraph says it 'would be odd to say his signature means nothing' and that consent 'remains important'; courts and voiding contracts are not discussed."),
    ("Consent always turns what would otherwise be wrong into something permissible, provided the person agreeing understands the terms and signs them of their own accord.",
     "This restates the usual view that the paragraph qualifies; it argues that consent's power 'depends on the conditions', including the alternatives available."),
    ("Employers who offer dangerous work to desperate people act wrongly, and the state should set minimum safety standards that no contract between them may lower.",
     "The paragraph makes no prescription about safety standards and does not condemn such employers outright; it argues about what consent can justify.")],
   "The paragraph states the common view of consent, qualifies it (power depends on conditions), grants the worker's consent some weight, and concludes that consent extracted under desperation shows preference over disaster rather than fairness, so it cannot justify on its own. The correct answer keeps the qualification and both halves of the conclusion.",
   "Choosing the 'not real consent' option, which overstates the critique and ignores the concession about the signature.",
   "V02L-consent-unequal-bargaining",
   "The correct answer must hold a concession and a limit together; the tempting distractor drops the concession.",
   cognitive="Analysis")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 025
ps("V02-L-025", "Philosophy and ethics",
   "Confidentiality serves the public by making disclosure possible, which is also why it yields to serious harm.",
   "Easy",
   "Professionals such as doctors and counsellors are bound to keep what their clients tell them confidential, and the duty is sometimes criticised as protecting secrets at others' expense. The criticism misunderstands its purpose. People disclose embarrassing symptoms, private fears and past mistakes only when they trust that the information will go no further, and without such disclosure the professional cannot help them, or help protect others from them. Confidentiality therefore serves the public as well as the client. This is also why the duty is not absolute: where keeping a secret would expose someone to serious and imminent harm, the reason for the duty, which was to make help possible, no longer supports silence.",
   "Confidentiality serves the public as well as clients by enabling disclosures that make help possible, which is why it yields when silence would permit serious, imminent harm.",
   [("Doctors and counsellors keep their clients' secrets mainly to protect their own reputations, which is why the duty of confidentiality is criticised as working against the public.",
     "The paragraph never mentions professionals' reputations; it says the duty exists to make help possible."),
    ("The duty of confidentiality must never be broken, since clients will stop telling professionals the truth as soon as they fear that their secrets may be revealed.",
     "The paragraph says the duty 'is not absolute' and gives way to serious and imminent harm, so an exceptionless duty contradicts it."),
    ("Clients tell doctors and counsellors about embarrassing symptoms and private fears only when they trust that the information will be kept from everyone else.",
     "This is a premise of the argument; it leaves out the conclusions that the duty serves the public and that it has limits.")],
   "The paragraph answers a criticism: confidentiality enables disclosure, disclosure enables help, so the duty serves the public too; and because its purpose is to make help possible, it yields when silence would cause serious harm. The correct answer gives both the purpose and the limit that follows from it.",
   "Choosing the 'never broken' option because the paragraph defends confidentiality, missing its final limit.",
   "V02L-duty-of-confidentiality",
   "The argument and its limit are each signalled ('therefore', 'This is also why').",
   cognitive="Understanding")
